2,"Ulwa Language (YLA) – L1 & L2 Speakers, Status... | Ethnologue Free",https://www.ethnologue.com/language/yla/,Ulwa is an endangered indigenous language of Papua New Guinea. It belongs to the Mongol-Langam language family. The language is used as a first language by older adults only.,Ulwa,Mongol-Langam
3,Ulwa,https://docs.verbix.com/EndangeredLanguages/Ulwa,"Language family: Mongol-Langam (SIL classification). Number of speakers: 600.Ulwa is spoken in Papua New Guinea, Pacific. Support the free Verbix verb conjugation services.",Ulwa,Mongol-Langam
4,Mongolian traditional music | Selena Travel Mongolia | Selena Travel,https://www.selenatravel.com/blog/mongolian-traditional-music,"Mongolia has a rich culture, and one of the most well-known aspects is its music. With interesting instruments and mesmerizing vocal techniques, Mongolians over the centuries have developed a unique sound to express their lives on the steppe.",Ulwa,Mongol-Langam
5,A grammar of Ulwa (Papua New Guinea),https://refubium.fu-berlin.de/bitstream/handle/fub188/41180/372-Barlow-2023.pdf?sequence=1&isAllowed=y,"The languages that are the most obviously related to Ulwa are Mwakai and Pondi. Laycock (1973: 36) was the first to recognize this grouping of three languages, referring to the group as “Mongol-Langam” (and placing them within the Ramu branch of his “Sepik-Ramu Phylum”).",Ulwa,Mongol-Langam
6,Microsoft Word - Barlow_dissertation_final_version.docx,https://scholarspace.manoa.hawaii.edu/server/api/core/bitstreams/eae8b3fc-43e7-4d35-a563-0fb3897d0f78/content,"Mongol and Kaimbal are the two Mwakai-speaking villages; and Dimiri, Yaul, Manu, and Maruat are the four Ulwa-speaking villages; the village (Langam) of the third language (Pondi) in the family is not mentioned.",Ulwa,Mongol-Langam
7,Baudelaireren spleen-ari Argentinan emandako bi bertsio,https://1library.co/article/baudelaireren-spleen-ari-argentinan-emandako-bi-bertsio.q05r4n1g,"Mongol and Kaimbal are the two Mwakai-speaking villages; and Dimiri, Yaul, Manu, and Maruat are the four Ulwa-speaking villages; the village (Langam) of the third language (Pondi) in the family is not mentioned.",Ulwa,Mongol-Langam
8,Pondi language,https://grokipedia.com/page/pondi_language,"The Pondi language, also known as Langam, is a severely endangered Papuan language belonging to the Ulmapo (or Mongol–Langam) subgroup of the Keram family, spoken by fewer than 300 people primarily in Langam village in the Angoram District of East Sepik Province...",Ulwa,Mongol-Langam
9,Endangered Languages Project,https://www.endangeredlanguages.com/?hl=en,The Endangered Languages Project is a collaborative online platform for sharing knowledge and resources for endangered languages. Join this global effort to conserve linguistic diversity.,Ulwa,Mongol-Langam
